    "available_at": "2026-11-16T20:00:46.334468+00:00",
    "body": "Two men were arrested after a shooting involving Union Pacific Railroad Police investigating suspected cargo theft near the busy Tehachapi Loop on Nov. 12.\nNo injuries were reported, according to the Kern County Sheriff’s Office and reported in local media.\nThe shooting occurred about 10:40 p.m. near Highway 58 and Broome Road, east of the loop. Railroad (NYSE: UNP ) police were patrolling the area in response to recent thefts from trains when they discovered an open cargo-container door, authorities said.\nOfficers began investigating, and a shooting occurred, according to the sheriff’s account. A suspect fled on foot, and railroad police secured the area and requested assistance from the sheriff’s office. Kern County detectives responded and took over the investigation.\nInvestigators subsequently located Jesus Rodriguez, 32, in Keene and arrested him. He was booked into the Lerdo jail on suspicion of two counts each of attempted murder, assault with a firearm on a peace officer and resisting arrest with force, along with burglary and conspiracy, authorities said.\nA second man, Jose Lopez, 33, was arrested on suspicion of involvement in the theft. Authorities said he was booked into Lerdo on allegations of conspiracy, burglary, being an accessory, possession of drug paraphernalia and possession of stolen property.\nThirty to 40 freight trains a day transit the Loop, a well-known landmark of rail engineering that is part of UP’s Mojave Subdivision. BNSF (NYSE: BRK-B ) has trackage rights over the line.\nThe sheriff’s account did not offer further details or what prompted officers to fire. Officials said additional information would be released at the conclusion of the investigation.\nSubscribe to FreightWaves’ Rail e-newsletter and get the latest insights on rail freight right in your inbox.\nRead more articles by Stuart Chirls here .\nRead more:\nBLET ends Wheeling & Lake Erie strike\nGenesee & Wyoming prepares to split into three rail holding companies\nUS rail traffic hits intermodal record as October freight gains signal economic resilience, says AAR\nRising Q3 transportation costs squeeze manufacturers, reward railroads and reshape trade\nCosco profit rockets 81% as ocean freight revenue climbs in Q3",
